<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  W4-A0 (#234) - the WiX MSI skeleton. Extended by W4-A2 with the W0 §4 SCM
  failure actions.

  W4-A0's claim: a WiX project in-tree produces an MSI that installs the
  accepted win-x64 server layout, creates the service `Tesserafin` with the
  `service` switch and the `docs/distribution/W0-windows-server.md` §4 argument
  list, and uninstalls the service and the binaries while leaving the state
  directories behind.

  W4-A2 adds ONE thing to that, and nothing else: the installed service carries
  the §4 recovery policy - restart after 60 s on the first and the second
  failure, no action on the third - and the hosted proof reads that policy back
  out of the Service Control Manager after the install rather than out of this
  file. See `docs/distribution/W4-A2-service-recovery.md`.

  What this authoring deliberately does NOT claim:

    * it is NOT a bit-identical MSI. W0 §5.6 measured that and accepted a
      bounded exception; W4-A0 makes no reproducibility claim at all;
    * it is NOT signed, and carries no certificate reference;
    * it does NOT start the service. W0 §10 is explicit that a fresh
      installation leaves the service "installed and enabled but not started";
    * it does NOT apply the §9.3 ACLs. Breaking inheritance at
      `%ProgramData%\Tesserafin\` and granting `NT SERVICE\Tesserafin` is a
      later W4 slice, and this package grants nothing;
    * it does NOT implement the rest of the §4 table. The 120 s stop timeout and
      the Event Log row are neither authored here nor measured, and W4-A2 rules
      on neither. The recovery row is the only one this slice adds;
    * it registers no Event Log source as a separate product;
    * it claims nothing about upgrade or repair beyond what `MajorUpgrade`
      emits by default.

  The `UpgradeCode` below is FROZEN by the W4-A1 owner ruling on #234. It is
  the GUID W4-A0 already authored, ratified as the 1.1 identity rather than
  re-minted:

    0f0c9f4e-1c5a-4b8e-9a3d-6d1f2b7c8e05

  ordinal, lowercase, no braces, exactly those bytes. It is the one value a
  later slice cannot change without stranding machines that already have the
  product installed, which is why it is pinned in the authoring and never
  generated per build. `ci/windows/w4/msi-controls.py` reddens any other GUID,
  and reddens these same digits written in a different case or wrapped in
  braces.

  The freeze reaches that one string and nothing else. The product name
  `Tesserafin Server`, the manufacturer `Tesserafin project` and the four
  retained-state component GUIDs are still only the identity this skeleton
  happened to build with; W4-A1 rules on none of them, and each still needs an
  explicit decision before 1.1 ships.

  Preprocessor inputs, all supplied by `ci/windows/w4/build-msi.ps1`:

    StageRoot   the extracted W2 package directory - the accepted layout,
                acquired through the frozen W2-A2 assembler, never restated
    HarvestRoot the same layout with the two files below held back, so that the
                harvest cannot deliver the server executable a second time
    Version     the MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH the commit already declares in
                SharedVersion.cs, the same source the assembler reads
    Mutation    `none` for the real package. Every other value builds a
                DELIBERATELY BROKEN package for one hostile control - four from
                W4-A0 over containment, the argument list and the uninstall, and
                three from W4-A2 over the recovery policy - and exists
                so those controls drive THIS authoring rather than a second
                copy of it written for the test - the same reason the frozen
                W2-A2 assembler carries its own PACK-ONLY parameter set.
                `ci/windows/w4/msi-controls.py` asserts the hosted acceptance
                build never passes anything but `none`.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">

  <?ifndef Mutation ?>
    <?define Mutation = "none" ?>
  <?endif ?>

  <!-- W4-A0 hostile control 1: "MSI does not contain tesserafin.exe". The
       component and the service registration are left intact so the control
       reddens containment and nothing else - a mutation that also removed the
       service would redden four predicates at once and be attributable to
       none of them. -->
  <?if $(var.Mutation) = "no-exe" ?>
    <?define ServerExeFileName = "tesserafin-w4control.exe" ?>
  <?else ?>
    <?define ServerExeFileName = "tesserafin.exe" ?>
  <?endif ?>

  <Package Name="Tesserafin Server"
           Manufacturer="Tesserafin project"
           Version="$(var.Version)"
           UpgradeCode="0f0c9f4e-1c5a-4b8e-9a3d-6d1f2b7c8e05"
           Scope="perMachine"
           Compressed="yes">

    <!-- W0 §10 names `MajorUpgrade` as what makes the upgrade deterministic and
         the downgrade refused. It is present because a package without it
         installs a second product side by side, which would be a worse default
         than no statement at all. W4-A0 exercises neither path and claims
         neither. -->
    <MajorUpgrade DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed." />

    <!-- mszip, not the LZX default. The payload is the whole self-contained
         server plus the Web tree plus the FFmpeg runtime - thousands of files
         and hundreds of megabytes - and the default compression level turns a
         packaging step into the longest step in the job. Compression is not a
         property this slice measures. -->
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" CompressionLevel="mszip" />

    <!-- W0 §9.1. Everything under INSTALLFOLDER is package-owned and replaced
         wholesale; everything under DATAFOLDER is operator-owned and never
         touched by the package. Both are public (upper-case) directory
         properties, so a caller can redirect either from the msiexec command
         line - which is how the hosted job installs under a disposable prefix
         instead of the runner's real %ProgramFiles%. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="TesserafinProgramFiles" Name="Tesserafin">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Server" />
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <StandardDirectory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="TesserafinProgramData" Name="Tesserafin">
        <Directory Id="DATAFOLDER" Name="Server">
          <Directory Id="DataConfigFolder" Name="config" />
          <Directory Id="DataDataFolder" Name="data" />
          <Directory Id="DataCacheFolder" Name="cache" />
          <Directory Id="DataLogFolder" Name="log" />
        </Directory>
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!-- The package-owned payload, harvested from the accepted W2 layout rather
         than enumerated here. An authored File element per member would be a
         second statement of the layout that could disagree with the archive,
         and there are thousands of them.

         The harvest reads HarvestRoot, which `ci/windows/w4/build-msi.ps1`
         materialises as the accepted stage MINUS two files. It is a separate
         directory rather than an Exclude list because the harvest is a LINKER
         behaviour: an exclude that did not match would deliver the executable
         twice into the same directory and fail an ICE, and it would fail on the
         runner rather than anywhere it could be checked first.

         `tesserafin.exe` is held back because it is authored explicitly below:
         the service registration has to live on the component that owns the
         executable, and a harvested component cannot carry one. It is still
         sourced from the stage, so the harvested payload and the authored
         executable cannot come from different packages.

         `tesserafin-server-service.ps1` is held back because W0 §6 gives it to
         the portable ZIP as a convenience over the same §4 contract and is
         explicit that it "is **not** a second installer". Shipping it inside
         the MSI would put a second registration path on a machine that already
         has the packaged one. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="PackagePayload" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Files Include="$(var.HarvestRoot)\**" />
    </ComponentGroup>

    <ComponentGroup Id="ServerHost" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="ServerExecutable" Guid="3c7a1f92-0b64-4e2d-8c19-5a4e7d0b6f31">
        <File Id="TesserafinServerExe"
              Name="$(var.ServerExeFileName)"
              Source="$(var.StageRoot)\tesserafin.exe"
              KeyPath="yes" />

        <!-- W0 §4, the service contract, stated once and in full.

             `Start="auto"` plus `ServiceConfig/@DelayedAutoStart` is the
             "Automatic (Delayed Start)" row. WiX answers WIX1149 for the core
             ServiceConfig element - the Windows Installer SDK documents parts
             of it as not working as expected - so the hosted proof does not
             take the authoring's word for it: it reads
             `DelayedAutostart` back out of the service's own registry key
             after the install.

             There is deliberately NO `Start="install"` on ServiceControl. W0
             §5.2 recorded that starting the service inside the transaction
             failed with 1920 and rolled the whole install back to 1603, hiding
             every other outcome behind one identity problem; W0 §10 wants the
             service installed and enabled but not started anyway.

             The `webdir` and `ffmpeg` arguments (each spelled with two leading
             hyphens, which an XML comment cannot contain) are always explicit,
             exactly as the Linux unit is, so the service can never fall back to a PATH encoder or a
             stale web directory. The `nowebclient` argument is never used. -->
        <?if $(var.Mutation) = "no-service-flag" ?>
          <!-- W4-A0 hostile control 2: the service binPath lacks the `service` switch. -->
          <ServiceInstall Id="TesserafinService"
                          Name="Tesserafin"
                          DisplayName="Tesserafin Server"
                          Description="Tesserafin media server. Manage it at http://localhost:8096."
                          Type="ownProcess"
                          Start="auto"
                          ErrorControl="normal"
                          Account="NT SERVICE\Tesserafin"
                          Vital="yes"
                          Arguments="--configdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]config&quot; --datadir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]data&quot; --cachedir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]cache&quot; --logdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]log&quot; --webdir &quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]web&quot; --ffmpeg &quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]ffmpeg\bin\ffmpeg.exe&quot;">
            <ServiceConfig DelayedAutoStart="yes" OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" />
            <ServiceConfigFailureActions OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" ResetPeriod="86400">
              <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
              <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
              <Failure Action="none" Delay="0" />
            </ServiceConfigFailureActions>
          </ServiceInstall>
        <?elseif $(var.Mutation) = "no-path-flags" ?>
          <!-- W4-A0 hostile control 3: the binPath lacks `ffmpeg` and `webdir`. -->
          <ServiceInstall Id="TesserafinService"
                          Name="Tesserafin"
                          DisplayName="Tesserafin Server"
                          Description="Tesserafin media server. Manage it at http://localhost:8096."
                          Type="ownProcess"
                          Start="auto"
                          ErrorControl="normal"
                          Account="NT SERVICE\Tesserafin"
                          Vital="yes"
                          Arguments="--service --configdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]config&quot; --datadir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]data&quot; --cachedir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]cache&quot; --logdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]log&quot;">
            <ServiceConfig DelayedAutoStart="yes" OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" />
            <ServiceConfigFailureActions OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" ResetPeriod="86400">
              <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
              <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
              <Failure Action="none" Delay="0" />
            </ServiceConfigFailureActions>
          </ServiceInstall>
        <?else ?>
          <ServiceInstall Id="TesserafinService"
                          Name="Tesserafin"
                          DisplayName="Tesserafin Server"
                          Description="Tesserafin media server. Manage it at http://localhost:8096."
                          Type="ownProcess"
                          Start="auto"
                          ErrorControl="normal"
                          Account="NT SERVICE\Tesserafin"
                          Vital="yes"
                          Arguments="--service --configdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]config&quot; --datadir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]data&quot; --cachedir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]cache&quot; --logdir &quot;[DATAFOLDER]log&quot; --webdir &quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]web&quot; --ffmpeg &quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]ffmpeg\bin\ffmpeg.exe&quot;">
            <ServiceConfig DelayedAutoStart="yes" OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" />
            <!-- W0 section 4, the recovery row: restart after 60 s on the first and
                 the second failure, and NO action on the third, so a crash loop is
                 visible rather than hidden. ResetPeriod is the SCM's own failure
                 counter reset, 86400 s, and Delay is milliseconds, which is why 60 s
                 is written 60000.

                 The third entry is load-bearing and is not decoration. The SCM
                 repeats the LAST configured action for every failure beyond the
                 length of the array, so a two-entry array of restarts restarts
                 forever - the exact outcome W0 section 4 refuses. An explicit
                 SC_ACTION_NONE third entry is what makes the loop stop and become
                 visible, and `third-action-restart` below is the control that
                 proves this authoring is what produces it.

                 WiX answers WIX1149 for this element too, for the same reason it
                 answers it for ServiceConfig, so the hosted proof does not take the
                 authoring's word for any of it: it reads the failure policy back out
                 of the Service Control Manager with QueryServiceConfig2W after the
                 install, and grades THAT. -->
            <?if $(var.Mutation) = "no-failure-actions" ?>
              <!-- W4-A2 hostile control 1: no failure actions authored at all. The
                   SCM then takes its default, which is to do nothing on any failure,
                   and W0 section 4's first and second restart never happen. -->
            <?elseif $(var.Mutation) = "first-action-not-restart" ?>
              <!-- W4-A2 hostile control 2: the first failure is not restart/60000.
                   It is still a restart, and still an authored one, so a gate that
                   only asked which ACTION the SCM recorded would call this correct;
                   the delay is the half that changed. -->
              <ServiceConfigFailureActions OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" ResetPeriod="86400">
                <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="1000" />
                <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
                <Failure Action="none" Delay="0" />
              </ServiceConfigFailureActions>
            <?elseif $(var.Mutation) = "third-action-restart" ?>
              <!-- W4-A2 hostile control 3: the third failure restarts, so the crash
                   loop W0 section 4 wants visible is hidden behind an indefinite
                   restart. Every other row of the policy is correct, which is what
                   makes this control attributable to the third entry alone. -->
              <ServiceConfigFailureActions OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" ResetPeriod="86400">
                <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
                <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
                <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
              </ServiceConfigFailureActions>
            <?else ?>
              <ServiceConfigFailureActions OnInstall="yes" OnReinstall="yes" ResetPeriod="86400">
                <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
                <Failure Action="restartService" Delay="60000" />
                <Failure Action="none" Delay="0" />
              </ServiceConfigFailureActions>
            <?endif ?>
          </ServiceInstall>
        <?endif ?>

        <!-- Removing the service is a property of the package, not of a custom
             action that could be skipped. W4-A0 hostile control 4 - "uninstall
             leaves the service registered" - is this element's absence. -->
        <?if $(var.Mutation) != "no-service-remove" ?>
          <ServiceControl Id="TesserafinServiceControl"
                          Name="Tesserafin"
                          Stop="both"
                          Remove="uninstall"
                          Wait="yes" />
        <?endif ?>
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!-- W0 §9.1 and §10: the operator-owned directories. They are created by
         the package so that "uninstall does not delete them" is a measurable
         statement rather than a vacuous one, and they are `Permanent` so that
         the retained-data policy is expressed IN THE PACKAGE - an ordinary
         uninstall cannot remove them, and no custom action has to be trusted
         not to.

         `NeverOverwrite` keeps a reinstall or a repair from touching a
         directory whose contents belong to the operator.

         The registry KeyPath is the W0 §5.2 probe's shape: a component whose
         KeyPath is a directory is evaluated by directory existence, which makes
         `Permanent` and `NeverOverwrite` behave unpredictably. Nothing reads
         these values; they exist to give each component a stable identity. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="RetainedState" Directory="DATAFOLDER">
      <Component Id="RetainedConfig" Directory="DataConfigFolder" Guid="9d2b7c05-4a18-4f63-b0e7-2c8a51d94f76" Permanent="yes" NeverOverwrite="yes">
        <CreateFolder />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\RetainedState" Name="config" Value="retained" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="RetainedData" Directory="DataDataFolder" Guid="4e81a63f-7d29-4c05-9b6a-8f3e0d27c1b4" Permanent="yes" NeverOverwrite="yes">
        <CreateFolder />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\RetainedState" Name="data" Value="retained" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="RetainedCache" Directory="DataCacheFolder" Guid="b7f04c18-3e5d-4a92-8c71-0d6b2f9e5a83" Permanent="yes" NeverOverwrite="yes">
        <CreateFolder />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\RetainedState" Name="cache" Value="retained" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="RetainedLog" Directory="DataLogFolder" Guid="5a3d9e26-8b47-4f10-a6c2-1e7f4b0d38c9" Permanent="yes" NeverOverwrite="yes">
        <CreateFolder />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Tesserafin\Server\RetainedState" Name="log" Value="retained" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="Tesserafin Server" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="PackagePayload" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ServerHost" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="RetainedState" />
    </Feature>
  </Package>
</Wix>
